Atypical Facial Pain (Persistent Idiopathic Facial Pain)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Unremitting non-anatomical facial pain, Absence of focal neurological deficits, Normal maxillofacial MRI

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-001
Reason for Visit: Severe, unremitting facial pain with acute psychological distress requiring urgent exclusion of acute neurological pathology and immediate symptom stabilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified sudden exacerbation of continuous facial pain with escalating anxiety and inability to cope. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out acute structural, demyelinating, or neoplastic aetiologies and to initiate immediate pharmacological and behavioral management to prevent further psychological deterioration and unnecessary presentation to the emergency department before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face feels like it's burning and being crushed at the same time, I can't take this anymore."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 48-hour severe exacerbation of a chronic, unremitting aching and throbbing facial pain. The pain is poorly localized, deep, and does not conform to a classic peripheral nerve distribution. It began as a heavy pressure and has escalated to a continuous burning sensation deep within the face. The patient notes the intensity has fluctuated but sharply worsened following significant recent psychological stress at work.
Functional Status: Independent but currently incapacitated by pain and associated high anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe mood disturbance and exhaustion; spouse expresses carer fatigue and concern regarding the patient's acute distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Appears acutely distressed, tense posture, frequently rubbing the affected side of the face.
Physical Exam: Symmetrical facial movements with entirely normal masticatory muscle tone. Cranial nerve examination reveals complete absence of focal neurological deficits and no objective sensory loss. Oral mucosa and dental structures are entirely normal on detailed clinical inspection. Documented history of normal maxillofacial MRI.
Environmental Observations: House is tidy but poorly lit; multiple empty over-the-counter analgesic packets visible on the kitchen counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against digital health record. Currently overusing paracetamol and ibuprofen without relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Atypical Facial Pain (Persistent Idiopathic Facial Pain) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out due to continuous aching nature rather than episodic, shock-like pain.

Dental Abscess - Ruled out due to completely normal oral mucosa, lack of localized dental tenderness, and normal temperature.

Temporal Arteritis - Ruled out due to absence of temporal artery tenderness, normal vision, and poorly localized presentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, acute-on-chronic exacerbation of unremitting, non-anatomical facial pain accompanied by significant acute mood disturbance. The intensity of the pain and the escalating psychological distress posed an immediate risk of self-harm or inappropriate emergency department presentation. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to definitively rule out emergent focal neurological deficits, structural pathology, and infection. Given the level of distress and the inefficacy of over-the-counter analgesia, immediate medical intervention and comprehensive reassurance could not be safely delayed until the patient's routine GP was available the following day. This encounter strictly satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours assessment (MBS Item 597).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cease ineffective over-the-counter NSAIDs. Commence low-dose Amitriptyline 10mg nocte for neuromodulation and sleep support.
Positive Behavioral Change: When pain spikes occur, immediately engage in a pre-planned 5-minute deep breathing exercise. Successfully completing this reduces physiological arousal, rewarding the behavior with improved pain tolerance and a sense of control.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Psychology referral for cognitive behavioral therapy focusing on pain management.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to titrate Amitriptyline and review psychological status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if developing sudden facial droop, visual loss, severe intractable vomiting, slurred speech, or acute suicidal ideation.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis relies on the clinical exclusion of acute structural causes; underlying conditions may evolve requiring further specialist investigation.
Clinical Course: Atypical facial pain is a chronic condition that fluctuates with stress. Complete resolution is rare, but significant symptomatic control is achievable with appropriate neuromodulation and psychological support.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you experience sudden weakness on one side of your face, loss of vision, or if the pain changes to sharp, electric-shock sensations.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Amitriptyline is used here not for depression, but to alter how the nerves in your face transmit pain signals to your brain, breaking the cycle of chronic pain.
Actionable Steps: Take the medication 2 hours before bed every night. Reward your adherence by tracking your sleep quality, which will reinforce the habit of nightly administration as your rest improves.

